From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 17:06:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] null renderer: software fragment shaders run in 2x2 quads,
 branching per lane

A fragment shader's 16 lanes are now 8 columns by 2 rows of pixels: four quads, with
helper lanes where a quad is partly outside the triangle. Shuffles, FSWZADD, votes and
derivatives see the quads as a subgroup, so the derivatives NVN's code works out are
right, and textures sampled with an implicit level of detail pick their level from the
quad: mipmaps work.

Programs that branch no longer run a pixel at a time: lanes that branch apart each go
their own way, the earliest instruction first, rejoining where they meet; results are
kept only for the lanes that ran an instruction. Local memory is per lane.

The texture fast paths take repeating (power-of-two) and clamped coordinates, do each
mip level a group of lanes at a time with trilinear blending, and fall back to one lane
at a time only for the lanes they can't do.
